course: dictionary
lesson: 10479
title: Words that name clothing and faith
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Context means the words around a word. They often give you clues about its meaning. Here, the word is turban.
explain: «Religious clothing like a headscarf, turban or kippah is important to the people who wear it, so treat it as normal.» The word «like» shows that a turban is one kind of religious clothing, alongside a headscarf and a kippah. So a turban is something people wear for their faith.
explain: «Initiated Sikhs, who have made special promises in a ceremony, wear the five Ks, including uncut hair, often under a turban.» The turban is worn over the hair, and the sentence tells you it is part of Sikh faith. When you meet a word like this, check the sentence for clues, and always treat people's faith and clothing with respect.
fact: A turban is a long piece of cloth wound around the head.
fact: Turbans are worn by men and some women in many cultures, especially by Sikhs as part of their faith.
fact: Sikhs wear a turban to cover their uncut hair, as a sign of faith, equality and respect.
fact: Turbans are also worn in parts of the Middle East, Africa and South Asia.
fact: Never touch or tease someone's head covering, because it is important to them.
exercise: «Religious clothing like a headscarf, turban or kippah is important to the people who wear it.» What is a turban? | A type of shoe | A small hat with a brim for the sun | A pair of warm gloves | *A long piece of cloth wound around the head
exercise: Why do many Sikhs wear a turban? | To keep the sun off their faces only | To hide a school badge | To look taller in photos | *To cover their uncut hair, as a sign of faith
exercise: Someone in your class wears a turban for their faith. What should you do? | Pull it off to see their hair | Tease them about it at lunch | *Treat it as normal and show respect | Ask them to take it off so they look the same as everyone
test: Besides Sikh communities, where are turbans also worn? | Only in outer space | Only in Scotland | *Parts of the Middle East, Africa and South Asia | Only on school trips
test: Read this: «Turbans are worn by men and some women.» What does the word «some» tell you? | Every woman wears one | *Not all women wear them, only a few do | Women never wear them | Turbans are only for children
exam: Why should you never touch or tease someone's head covering? | Because it is a costume meant to be laughed at | Because it is always made of paper | Because it is only for school plays | *Because it is part of their faith and deserves respect
